{
  "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
  "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nJEFFREY L. FISHER, ESQ., Stanford, Cal.; on behalf of Petitioner.\nWILLIAM B. COLLINS, ESQ., Deputy Solicitor General, Olympia, Wash.; on behalf of Respondent.\nMATTHEW D. ROBERTS, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for United States, as amicus curiae, supporting Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FISHER: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nIn Sharp versus Pain, the Ninth Circuit opinion that first decided the question that's before you today, the Ninth Circuit said, and I'm quoting:\n\"The rule of Blakely that the statutory maximum is the maximum sentence a judge may impose solely on the basis of the facts reflected in the jury verdict, was not clear until the Blakely decision itself.\" The Ninth Circuit is simply wrong. In this Court's Apprendi decision it laid down precisely that rule. At page 483 of that decision this Court described the statutory maximum concept as, quote, \"the maximum a defendant would receive if punished according to the facts reflected in the jury verdict alone\" -- virtually the identical language."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is the decision which the judge makes here to sentence consecutively rather than concurrently, a decision that requires the finding of any fact about the commission of the crimes themselves or the circumstances of those crimes or about the defendant's character?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I thought the only fact that had to be found was that, given the classification sentencing scheme Washington had, there would be, in effect, a free crime, no incremental punishment, unless there were consecutive sentencing. That's not a fact that falls within any of those categories of crime, character, or circumstances."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "a new Washington Court of Appeals decision that considers a consecutive sentences imposed exactly the same way that Mr. Burton's was. That is to say, they are run consecutively based on the clearly too lenient factor.\nThat case is called State versus Washington and it was just reported at 143 P.3d 606, 143 P.3d 606. The Washington Court of Appeals in that case, considering a sentence just like Mr. Burton's, says that it does trigger and violate Blakely."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But, Mr. Fisher, even if it's true that there are other examples out there that might qualify for that example, it's not true of this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Apprendi-Blakely line of cases dealing with consecutive sentences. But what you've done is laid down a rule from the very State that we're dealing with here that says that if the judge needs to make an extra finding beyond the elements of the crimes of conviction and beyond the facts encompassed in the jury's finding of guilt for those crimes, then those findings need to be proved to a jury beyond a reasonable doubt."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Fisher, there's another potential impediment in this case and I would like you to comment on it. That is the petition from the sentence, it was second in time. There was a prior petition that challenged just the conviction, and under the governing statute, to have a second petition, you've got to get permission from the court of appeals and it has to meet stringent criteria."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0000-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FISHER: I think that's fair to say, Mr. Chief Justice. Because in Blakely all you needed to do was apply Apprendi which said that if you have two different statutory thresholds, the pertinent threshold for Sixth Amendment and Fourteenth Amendment purposes is the one that cabins the judge's discretion based on the facts in the jury verdict. To decide Booker this Court had to take the term statutory maximum and apply that to a different type of threshold, which was as this Court put it a court rule or a quasi legislative enactment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "took the state's argument and rejected it by saying our precedents on this point are clear. And it just simply quoted the Apprendi language, that the statutory maximum for Sixth and Fourteenth Amendment purposes is the maximum that a defendant may receive based on the facts and the jury verdict alone."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "none of them -- not a single one -- quotes Apprendi or even acknowledges the passages in Apprendi that said the test is not one of form but of effect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "was the very guts of the holding of Apprendi."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is there any precedent that says that's how it's read?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SOUTER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, Mr. Collins, isn't it your position that, number one, the only judgment that he can attack on habeas is the judgment that is extant at the time of the habeas proceeding, and that is the '92 judgment that follows the resentencing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice STEVENS",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I just be sure I understand one thing about that? So you're saying at the time he filed the petition on December 28, 1998, he had already had, the second judgment had already been entered by the Washington Supreme Court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why does it make any less sense to allow separate habeas challenges to, first, the conviction, and then the sentence, than it does to allow separate appeals to this Court from each of those?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "this a successive -- is this a successive petition, and we would say it is, because he filed the first one and then the second one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0001-0009",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I thought the law was that the sentence shall be concurrent unless, and the unless is that the judge makes an additional finding, the very same kind of finding that he would make in determining aggravating factor."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Was it logical for them to conclude it or were they -- were they expressing the hope that the Court would draw a distinction which it had not drawn in the formulation that it gave in Apprendi? It's one thing to say that if you draw no further distinctions, Apprendi requires a certain result. It's another thing to say but maybe they will draw a distinction and we're going to predict that they will, and hence not apply Apprendi."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay, but doesn't that require drawing a distinction that Apprendi did not speak to?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "please the Court: If I could begin by addressing the issues that you were just talking about, there was a distinction that the Court could have drawn between Apprendi and Blakely and that reasonable jurists could have drawn and did draw."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "amicus brief in Blakely and it rested on the fact that sentencing guideline systems like Washington try to channel but not to eliminate the discretion that sentencing judges have to sentence within the otherwise applicable limits. And in the Washington system the sentencing judge retained a significant degree of discretion that reasonable jurists could have analogized to traditional sentencing systems that aren't constrained by Apprendi."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "reasonable jurist could have accepted those distinctions and drawn a difference, and we submit that they could have."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Is that true in fact?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Jeffrey L. Fisher)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f2841bde5fe76e139787612a7269a63e05369e469d92506564643bb02e617743",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f2841bde5fe7-0002-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "On jurisdiction, I think Mr. Roberts is right that this rarely happens in the Federal system. It rarely happens in the State system. We can't find any other case where a petitioner has gone in naming, as the State would say, in effect the wrong judgment and saying: \"I'm challenging this judgment.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9222",
